To create a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text, you can use a library like `markdowncpp`. First, ensure you have the `markdowncpp` library installed and included in your project. Here's a simple implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdowncpp/markdown.h>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdowncpp::Markdown md;
    std::string htmlText = md.convert(markdownText);
    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Hello World\n\nThis is some *markdown* text";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdownText << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

